# On The Gravitational Nature Of Time

<Hint label="Update"> This is a summary of the more academic article available [here](/paper). If you're on a **Mac**, you'll probably get more value out of the interactive demo [here](https://github.com/flusterIO/aog_explorer/releases/tag/0.0.3). The article you're looking at now is kind of a work in progress as it's being rewritten.</Hint>

<Admonition title="What is this?">

This is both a sample note representing what Conundrum is capable of and a short summary of a modified model of relativity that inspired Conundrum and Fluster; let me explain...

It was early 2021 when I was putting aside my software work for the night at 3am. I had put in some toast when I opened a book I had sitting there, a book of Einstein's lectures. I flipped to a random page when something stood out to me: Einstein's famous quote on the length of moving rods from "On The Electrodynamics of Moving Bodies".

Maybe it was just seeing that line out of context that triggered something in me, but I realized that Einstein's insistence on a static (non-expanding) Universe early in his career led him to make certain assumptions that I feel should be revisited in the context of cosmic inflation.

I quit my job as a software engineer to work on this model full time, studying physics while living on the side of the street with my dog but I think I have it: Classically synchronous General Relativity.

</Admonition>

## The Lighthouse & The Clock-Tower
> The thought experiment that started it all

Let there be two observers, $A$ and $B$. Let $A$ and $B$ agree that $B$ should travel between two arbitrary points in space, $p_1$ and $p_2$ at some relativistic velocity $v$. At $p_2$ place a time keeping device large enough to be visible from $p_1$ where $A$ remains at relative rest.

At what time does $B$ reach $p_2$?

That extra clock at $p_2$ complicates things, no? It should.

While this can technically be resolved by claiming that a clock travelling with $B$ would measure $\Delta t = \gamma\frac{d}{v}$, I take the position that it is absolutely absurd to dilate the clock moving with $B$ while the clock at $p_2$ continues to tick at an inertial pace. How can one process ($A$ transitioning between $p_1$ and $p_2$) as measured by $A$ be different from that elapsed period according to $B$? 

This is 100% analogous to conducting the simple trigonometric parallax experiment, "Put your thumb over the light switch in the corner of the room" and the light switch _actually_ moving when you swap your hand over to the other eye.


### Unwrapping Kaluza-Klein

<Image src="/assets/diagrams/paper/trig_deriv_of_y.png" maxHeight="smedium" centerSelf/>

<Card title="Where things stand" desc="I'll be upfront about this as we go, but here's the scorecard" sidebar right>


**Solid (given the vacuum equations of GR):** the Lorentz factor as the slope of a fourth spatial coordinate, the geometry of Schwarzschild and of the whole expanding Universe from that one slope, gravitational time dilation falling out of the same picture, and a neat link between a falling apple and cosmic expansion. <br />

**A hypothesis (with predictions I can be proven wrong by):** inertia is Machian and switches regime at an acceleration around $cH_0$. I test it against the Milky Way below. <br />

**Number fitting:** a handful of coincidences I find fun and can't justify. They're clearly labeled.
</Card>

Consider the figure above where $i = 1, 2, 3$. As in the [paper](/paper), $\mathbf{U}$ represents 


$$
\partial_y \mathcal{H} = \mathcal{F} \left( x_{i}, y \right)
$$

where $\mathbf{S}$ and $\mathbf{U}$ represent an embedded surface within this 4-dimensional hyperspace, $\mathcal{H}$ giving the functions of $\mathbb{R}^{3}$ space

$$
\mathbf{S} \left( x_{i} \right) = \mathcal{F} \left( x_{i}, \mathbf{S}_{y} \right) \quad , \quad \mathbf{U} \left( x_{i} \right) = \mathcal{F} \left( x_{i}, \mathbf{U}_{y} \right)
$$

This is the geometry we'll use throughout this derivation.


> This is to be understood similary to the relationship between the surface of a sphere and it's volume, only our $\mathbb{R}^{3}$ volume is equivalent to the surface of an $\mathbb{R}^{4}$ space!

We then end up with 

$$
\boxed{
\partial_\tau y = \frac{v^{2}}{\sqrt{c^{2} - v^{2}}}
} \qquad \boxed{
\partial_x y = \frac{v}{\sqrt{c^{2} - v^{2}}}
}
$$

Which remains closed since 

$$
v \left( \gamma \right) \partial_x y = \partial_\tau y
$$


where

$$
v \left( \gamma \right) = c \sqrt{1 - \frac{1}{\gamma^{2}}}
$$
or the Lorentz factor solved for $v$.

We can then solve this trigonometrically to find

$$
\boxed{
\gamma = \sqrt{1 + \left( \partial_x y \right)^{2}}
}
$$

### The Metric

This then gives a simplified metric of

$$
G_{AB}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
g_{\mu\nu} & 0\\
0 & G_{44}
\end{pmatrix}
$$

for the line element

$$
dS^2
=
g_{\mu\nu}dx^\mu dx^\nu
+
G_{44}dy^2
$$

And since the force is radial, for a static source $G_{44} = 1$, we find 

$$
\Gamma^R_{44}
=
-\frac{1}{2}g^{RR}
\frac{\partial G_{44}}{\partial R}.
$$
